window.docContents[73].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"Introduce the bearer: the particular system a quantity is about. The context has two black-box oscillators, A and B, each a body on a wall spring. An IndividualQuantity o k ℝ is a real magnitude of kind k about the object o, so the oscillator a quantity is about is part of its type. Dividing oscillator A's spring constant by oscillator A's mass type-checks and gives an IndividualQuantity of kind omegaSquaredK about A. Supplying oscillator B's mass where a mass about A is expected fails to type-check with a type mismatch. A Designated instance gives A and B distinct nominal names, so they remain two systems even when their parameter values are equal.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.2.1.1. Purpose","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Synopsis--Purpose"}});